<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  **Also trust custom root certificates.**

  From Android 7, apps trust only the system store, not certificates the user
  installed. Anyone running Jellyfin behind a private certificate authority
  (not on the internet, so no Let's Encrypt) saw "The server's certificate is
  not trusted" although the browser on the same phone opened it fine.

  `user` is listed on purpose: the device owner installed it themselves, and
  without this entry a private server is unreachable.

  **Cleartext (http) stays allowed for every address, on purpose.**

  Jellyfin mostly runs at home without TLS: `http://192.168.…:8096`,
  `http://nas.local:8096`, a Tailscale name or a public address without a
  certificate. The user types the address freely, so a list of allowed hosts
  (`domain-config`) cannot exist, and this file does not know IP ranges such as
  192.168.0.0/16. A ban would have broken exactly these servers. Without a
  scheme the app uses https, on the home network http (`ServerURL` in the
  package). The rule lives only here, not additionally as
  `usesCleartextTraffic` in the manifest, which this file overrides from
  Android 7 anyway.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
